  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Lecture core · K-means assignment and update",
      "body": "K-means alternates $z_i\\leftarrow\\arg\\min_k\\lVert x_i-c_k\\rVert^2$ and $c_k\\leftarrow\\frac{1}{|C_k|}\\sum_{i:z_i=k}x_i$. Each step does not increase the within-cluster squared-distance objective, but the result depends on initialization and is generally only a local optimum.",
      "sourceType": "lecture",
      "teaching": {
        "concept": "One K-means iteration is a concrete alternating-minimization calculation: nearest-centroid assignment followed by a mean update.",
        "useWhen": "Use it for numerical questions and to verify that an update decreases or preserves the within-cluster objective.",
        "examMove": "Keep old centroids for assignment, then recompute each non-empty centroid from the newly assigned points.",
        "trap": "Mixing assignments from different iterations or dividing by an empty cluster silently changes the algorithm."
      },
      "math": {
        "latex": "J=\\sum_i\\lVert x_i-c_{z_i}\\rVert_2^2",
        "explanation": "The assignment chooses the closest current centroid; the centroid update is the minimizer of the squared distances for a fixed assignment.",
        "symbols": [
          {
            "latex": "z_i",
            "meaning": "hard assignment"
          },
          {
            "latex": "c_k",
            "meaning": "centroid"
          },
          {
            "latex": "J",
            "meaning": "K-means objective"
          }
        ],
        "name": "K-means assignment and centroid update",
        "purpose": "Use it to carry out one concrete iteration and verify which objective each step improves.",
        "sourceType": "lecture"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Textbook depth · GMM responsibilities",
      "body": "A GMM models $p(x)=\\sum_k\\pi_k\\mathcal{N}(x\\mid\\mu_k,\\Sigma_k)$. The E-step computes $\\gamma_{ik}=P(z_i=k\\mid x_i)$ by multiplying the prior and likelihood, then normalizing across components.",
      "sourceType": "textbook",
      "math": {
        "latex": "\\gamma_{ik}=\\frac{\\pi_k\\mathcal{N}(x_i\\mid\\mu_k,\\Sigma_k)}{\\sum_j\\pi_j\\mathcal{N}(x_i\\mid\\mu_j,\\Sigma_j)}",
        "explanation": "Responsibilities are soft labels and sum to one across components for each point. A component with a smaller prior can still receive high responsibility if its likelihood is much larger.",
        "symbols": [
          {
            "latex": "\\pi_k",
            "meaning": "mixture weight"
          },
          {
            "latex": "\\mu_k",
            "meaning": "component mean"
          },
          {
            "latex": "\\Sigma_k",
            "meaning": "component covariance"
          }
        ],
        "name": "GMM responsibility",
        "purpose": "Use it to compute the posterior membership weight of a point under each mixture component.",
        "sourceType": "textbook"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Textbook depth · M-step",
      "body": "Using responsibilities, define $N_k=\\sum_i\\gamma_{ik}$. Then update $\\pi_k=N_k/N$, $\\mu_k=\\frac1{N_k}\\sum_i\\gamma_{ik}x_i$, and the covariance as the responsibility-weighted scatter. The E and M steps form an alternating optimization of the latent-variable likelihood.",
      "sourceType": "textbook",
      "math": {
        "latex": "\\mu_k^{new}=\\frac{\\sum_i\\gamma_{ik}x_i}{\\sum_i\\gamma_{ik}}",
        "explanation": "The mean is a weighted average, so high-responsibility points pull the component more strongly.",
        "symbols": [
          {
            "latex": "N_k",
            "meaning": "effective component count"
          },
          {
            "latex": "\\gamma_{ik}",
            "meaning": "soft membership weight"
          }
        ],
        "name": "GMM M-step parameter update",
        "purpose": "Use it to refit mixture weights, means, or covariances using the responsibilities from the E-step.",
        "sourceType": "textbook"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Assessment habit · Keep probability and distance reasoning distinct",
      "body": "K-means chooses one nearest centroid using a distance. GMM compares weighted probability densities and can model covariance shape. A responsibility is not simply the inverse distance and it must sum to one over components for each point.",
      "sourceType": "ref"
    }
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "title": "Responsibility by hand",
      "steps": [
        "Let priors be $\\pi_1=0.6$ and $\\pi_2=0.4$, and likelihoods at x be 0.5 and 0.1.",
        "The unnormalized weights are 0.3 and 0.04.",
        "Normalize by 0.34: $\\gamma_1\\approx0.8824$ and $\\gamma_2\\approx0.1176$."
      ],
      "answer": "Always multiply by the prior before normalizing the component likelihoods.",
      "sourceType": "textbook"
    },
    {
      "title": "One M-step mean",
      "steps": [
        "Suppose scalar points are $x1=0$ and $x2=4$ with responsibilities 0.8 and 0.2 for component k.",
        "The effective count is $Nk=1.0$.",
        "The updated mean is ($0.8*0+0.2*4$)/1.0=0.8."
      ],
      "answer": "The M-step mean is a responsibility-weighted average, not an unweighted average of all data.",
      "sourceType": "textbook"
    }
  ],
  "criticalQuestions": [
    {
      "prompt": "Why is EM called an alternating procedure?",
      "focus": "latent variables",
      "angle": "separate E and M",
      "modelAnswer": "The E-step estimates latent memberships with current parameters; the M-step fits parameters with those memberships held as weights. Each step makes a different part of the coupled problem tractable."
    },
    {
      "prompt": "Why can repeated EM runs disagree?",
      "focus": "non-convexity",
      "angle": "connect initialization and local optimum",
      "modelAnswer": "The mixture likelihood is non-convex and can have local optima or degenerate regions. Different initial parameters can lead to different final solutions."
    }
  ],
